    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the excessive goblin references in my previous response; I am trying to be helpful with your F-150 transmission issue, not distract you with goblin folklore. You are right to focus on the technical side.\n\nRegarding the irregular pressure spikes in your 2022 F-150's automatic transmission, here are the most likely technical causes to investigate:\n\n1.  **Torque Converter Clutch (TCC) Solenoid:** This is the most common culprit for pressure fluctuations. If the solenoid is sticking, dirty, or failing to modulate correctly, it causes the TCC to engage and disengage abruptly, creating those spikes.\n2.  **Valve Body Issues:** Debris, worn spools, or a stuck valve inside the valve body can disrupt hydraulic flow, leading to erratic pressure readings.\n3.  **Low or Dirty Transmission Fluid:** Insufficient fluid volume or fluid that has degraded (lost viscosity or contains metal shavings) prevents the pump from maintaining steady pressure.\n4.  **Internal Pump Wear:** If the transmission pump is worn out, it may struggle to overcome internal resistance, causing pressure drops and spikes as it tries to compensate.",
